#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/694b10f1d1c18f25e079e9e7cc75e9cc457ef01d0c229f006308fd667108459b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/247c0a29294e61735b969d769d8ba3794c0b5e816c3968a8db087740eeb925d8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042225/assets/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822b1894-de17-5a39-b99c-38ebb8d1cf37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316047} {:answer/id #uuid "39875807-fe18-587b-8863-7346df9ccb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316048} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb8dbd8-ce6e-5f16-8b79-77be55ddeb4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316049} {:answer/id #uuid "610741b1-d059-5614-beb3-7a8eec8ec8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316050} {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6998-7559-5bed-9ec2-a1771dd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b78a6998-7559-5bed-9ec2-a1771dd67f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316051} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf90ac7-69d3-5fff-bf5a-ddb95afa0f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27d39cb2-4afd-51aa-ba9c-4ddc1b6ff266" :question/math-academy-id "q-130868" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12456346-b6a7-544d-ad76-1a777b9829e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ an $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316054} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e5bd9f-669b-5474-80ef-344d221820e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} {:answer/id #uuid "0f27e316-616c-5c96-aaea-3590c5a7f34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316056} {:answer/id #uuid "4afb4ada-2856-55cf-b68b-200a737f4bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316057} {:answer/id #uuid "2f438f20-12bf-5efc-9021-7b6f0bb59358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f438f20-12bf-5efc-9021-7b6f0bb59358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316058} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd8dd10c-0982-5496-9bfc-972d9275245b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d6c8932-9459-5228-9f76-3c00a801677b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131347" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1434626-7592-55e2-8c20-d8e0763453d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316061} {:answer/id #uuid "f220a910-1c8f-5ff5-9795-f881b26cc0fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316062} {:answer/id #uuid "2620eb0b-038f-5a5b-bc41-af20438232ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316063} {:answer/id #uuid "61a942d0-3f57-51be-934b-23a1d6f6f857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316064} {:answer/id #uuid "367cdc21-d31c-5c70-99dd-6a04769ac3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61a942d0-3f57-51be-934b-23a1d6f6f857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316064} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd019b54-0c3a-52c1-961b-b6d468db5ece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a618d522-1f89-5278-907e-8cb0fd49bf99" :question/math-academy-id "q-131381" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733b0531-2bf7-552e-8c9e-fb776f997362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316068} {:answer/id #uuid "46172b88-eceb-5e1c-bccc-e0b842cf34fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316069} {:answer/id #uuid "207851b4-98aa-5049-96b3-7e7af4812b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316070} {:answer/id #uuid "2765f195-a12d-5343-a730-a276085f4ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316071} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1ad314-e95e-5de0-810f-bc11a82ad065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "207851b4-98aa-5049-96b3-7e7af4812b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316070} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d08ae7-b14a-5ef2-91b5-d10ffcaea1a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd68e3fc-b0d0-52a2-ba1f-362abdda52e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-131386" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0add77f-f4a9-52b8-ac7e-0782ca701f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316075} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0d2e25-3329-595f-9c6d-1a1ae7bac970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316076} {:answer/id #uuid "905d066b-e2ca-5190-b661-5873cd339c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316077} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b33e8-f12d-54e7-b2d7-7ec6d92a3bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316078} {:answer/id #uuid "99a9f4d6-6be9-586f-9d55-3ac54729adfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "905d066b-e2ca-5190-b661-5873cd339c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316077} :answer-field/id #uuid "d397cac0-ceb4-58dc-9e71-50229d91b300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41dc19c4-f697-52b3-b9b5-76ded5157f14" :question/math-academy-id "q-131999" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58574ec8-399a-5f6e-b6f2-d2f9038956ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316082} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5e7730-2ce7-5404-a715-71e79b625181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316083} {:answer/id #uuid "47ad9c3b-7721-57b0-9963-ebd1e32af9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316084} {:answer/id #uuid "da5b93c8-c6e6-57ab-8524-d917ba5b90fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316085} {:answer/id #uuid "ad716d96-451e-55ce-9a8c-2f9fafbe2b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47ad9c3b-7721-57b0-9963-ebd1e32af9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316084} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9db52fb-f11f-5324-a6c0-f787d3b28169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eacd4d73-2c00-54b5-9aa2-14a56b16b0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-31871" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eab3c25-cc50-5c7d-bc59-a051c74256cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316089} {:answer/id #uuid "7c456437-90b5-5e39-ba4d-ae5ddfa501c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316090} {:answer/id #uuid "234be48b-228b-517f-803a-c07955b146f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316091} {:answer/id #uuid "60ac1883-2e34-5c35-97a1-ba1e506e83b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316092} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2da5c8-1943-5ad8-ac16-45c37c0ef121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "234be48b-228b-517f-803a-c07955b146f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316091} :answer-field/id #uuid "da349521-b51d-51e7-bf38-f7dd7a3726d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76bc14dd-df85-52d6-bc25-d8ef7d523eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-31946" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $U$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $U$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $U$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[U\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${U}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{U}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "594a541c-402a-5f3f-b5da-a9ce57d0d03a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316096} {:answer/id #uuid "94113f1a-7ebc-50f0-a44f-d4404be04496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316097} {:answer/id #uuid "c21085dd-9c94-5fa3-95a1-50de12846340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316098} {:answer/id #uuid "3f35fb7f-3c9b-5a00-9bad-0d1abfa0477f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316099} {:answer/id #uuid "8e505390-769c-5936-ab28-3dbcc86ca094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594a541c-402a-5f3f-b5da-a9ce57d0d03a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316096} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e3afab-6a81-523f-830c-204ce02f31a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fb0889d-6fed-5243-aaf7-929089854f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31954" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51423c48-2b3e-5e5e-9a14-21770b17f8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 15 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316103} {:answer/id #uuid "e22beadd-6fdb-55dc-b9da-17a915b5cc34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316104} {:answer/id #uuid "7753a90e-0f4d-5a25-a8ef-0d7aa8de1190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316105} {:answer/id #uuid "08b8cb02-9017-59c9-bf86-cc986ae4116f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316106} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d9bbff-9305-52a9-91be-99abab6b6f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08b8cb02-9017-59c9-bf86-cc986ae4116f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316106} :answer-field/id #uuid "906337a2-be08-5c1e-9e9c-8c229d5f6712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82bada1a-7aa7-561c-9255-4184b5c3658d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42987" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${C}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 15 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $C$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $C$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 15 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-7){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 15 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${C}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{C}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a5fa321-6c74-58fc-9ac2-5d3f87a9d1c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316109} {:answer/id #uuid "95786a23-4493-58a7-8fee-47fa72bf02fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316110} {:answer/id #uuid "a3be7fe5-ac31-5b50-a52b-3cb4698ab4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316111} {:answer/id #uuid "552c605b-4407-501a-af4e-7765a3c72991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316112} {:answer/id #uuid "19b41ec3-76e7-57ea-af12-4441dc1425ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3be7fe5-ac31-5b50-a52b-3cb4698ab4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316111} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a10aaa3-d3b6-5b52-983c-83c52abc2ee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ae71b84-85fc-5e7b-937d-d496882effbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-42988" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5272bedb-f5cb-5248-a6e2-8c4780c44589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316116} {:answer/id #uuid "794a79e9-007b-53ea-b9e4-21cbacc01099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117} {:answer/id #uuid "466f40a1-7d60-5832-81fb-9e44a5ac70a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316118} {:answer/id #uuid "789231b1-663a-52b6-8809-47c43cd39d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316119} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f7ca12-9fc7-5ffe-a773-210c53108e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3f7ca12-9fc7-5ffe-a773-210c53108e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316120} :answer-field/id #uuid "d27219ca-d5bc-5fb9-8469-6189d630c06d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5980ce24-2fb8-5a08-94d5-9900cc4fdcd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-43004" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $V$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $V$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdd50191-b935-53c1-b729-05293a954016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316123} {:answer/id #uuid "ee539306-dc16-5c6a-ac64-c80f90300a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316124} {:answer/id #uuid "f36e00ee-0bd4-52b0-ac41-d5b0c68c8ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316125} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc52ad0-43a8-5d07-a2e3-026c0d7bb13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316126} {:answer/id #uuid "c11f12e8-e827-59dd-bc86-d2966f0728ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee539306-dc16-5c6a-ac64-c80f90300a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316124} :answer-field/id #uuid "feeb800c-51ce-52bf-9086-c68c25f4bdc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08503628-ebe1-533e-ab77-0800c6924f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-75448" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4927c9f3-d4cc-55b0-af43-b8c0d90bcfaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316130} {:answer/id #uuid "973ffdfd-968b-59c9-8699-529f363de9f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316131} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e44a19-aa80-514c-b6d6-c9108f9e37cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316132} {:answer/id #uuid "aa809462-8786-5077-aaeb-dbd17df6e4a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316133} {:answer/id #uuid "ba3b7038-9810-596d-873a-58edbdfff711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "973ffdfd-968b-59c9-8699-529f363de9f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316131} :answer-field/id #uuid "881a44d8-94d0-582e-91bc-336126c5a6aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eb6d032-088d-565d-ab8d-72bcf173e9b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75508" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf4ca2a-9201-5e7c-8396-e5c8fc983548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316137} {:answer/id #uuid "d2790338-f319-5012-8eba-3c2442799bdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316138} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ec3e15-4bec-5fe8-a41c-87839772e5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316139} {:answer/id #uuid "5169b214-0d72-55f4-82c4-f186962a91a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316140} {:answer/id #uuid "2669cafc-8c17-521d-a6ef-03e7fa8f29ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2669cafc-8c17-521d-a6ef-03e7fa8f29ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316141} :answer-field/id #uuid "211e35b9-5f74-59ee-9424-816ac22e8b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf372bba-7e5e-585d-a9f1-baf4b638e989" :question/math-academy-id "q-75595" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c552824b-e24d-5fb3-821a-3a0dd043f6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316144} {:answer/id #uuid "398fdd89-5890-5788-aaa8-1a2c4c31962f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316145} {:answer/id #uuid "b657b32e-6798-5b1f-8c0c-cddb8cb717bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316146} {:answer/id #uuid "96831c25-b3df-50c9-98a8-0e9dcdbb6caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316147} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba49cca-3f6d-5df2-a9b6-9e766d7a856e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "398fdd89-5890-5788-aaa8-1a2c4c31962f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316145} :answer-field/id #uuid "337107cd-0f6d-5329-b3e7-d9f1158f1e67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8255e634-191f-51e8-9b24-327dee735c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75599" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"."}]}
